[32mAnswer #2: Consider a right triangle with legs a and b, and hypotenuse c.  Let the triangle be denoted as ABC, where C is the right angle. Draw an altitude from C to the hypotenuse, and let the point where it intersects the hypotenuse be D.  This creates two smaller right triangles, ADC and BDC.

Triangle ADC is similar to triangle ABC (they share angle A and both have a right angle).  Therefore, the ratio of corresponding sides is equal:  CD/AC = AC/AB, or CD/b = b/c, so CD = b²/c.

Likewise, triangle BDC is similar to triangle ABC.  Thus, CD/BC = BC/AB, or CD/a = a/c, so CD = a²/c.

The length of the hypotenuse AB is the sum of the lengths of AD and BD.
AD = AB - BD = c - BD
Also, from similar triangles ADC and ABC we have AD/AC = AC/AB, so AD/b = b/c, thus AD = b²/c
Similarly, from similar triangles BCD and ABC we have BD/BC = BC/AB, so BD/a = a/c, thus BD = a²/c.

Now, notice that the altitude divides the hypotenuse into two segments with lengths b²/c and a²/c.  The sum of these segments must equal the length of the hypotenuse, c:

a²/c + b²/c = c

Multiplying both sides by c gives:

a² + b² = c²

This completes the proof of the Pythagorean theorem.[0m
