[32mAnswer #1: Assume there are a finite number of primes, and let them be $p_1, p_2, \dots, p_n$. Consider the number $N = p_1 p_2 \cdots p_n + 1$.  $N$ is either prime or composite. If $N$ is prime, it is a new prime not in our list. If $N$ is composite, it must be divisible by some prime $p$. However, if $p$ is one of $p_1, p_2, \dots, p_n$, then it would divide $N - p_1 p_2 \cdots p_n = 1$, which is impossible. Thus, $p$ must be a new prime not in our original list.  Therefore, there are infinitely many primes.[0m
