[36mQuestion: Give me a proof to Euclid's theorem of the infinitude of primes.[0m
[32mAnswer #2: Suppose for the sake of contradiction that there are only finitely many prime numbers, say $p_1, p_2, \dots, p_n$.  Consider the number $N = p_1p_2\cdots p_n$. Since $N+1 > 1$, it must have a prime divisor $p$. If $p$ were one of $p_1, p_2, \dots, p_n$, then $p$ would divide both $N$ and $N+1$, so $p$ would divide their difference, $(N+1) - N = 1$.  But no prime number divides 1. This is a contradiction, which means there cannot be only finitely many prime numbers.  Therefore, there are infinitely many primes.[0m
[35mCoherence Score: 100[0m
