[36mQuestion: Give me a proof to Euclid's theorem of the infinitude of primes.[0m
[32mAnswer #3: Let $p_1, p_2, \dots, p_n$ be any finite set of primes.  Consider the number $N = 2^{p_1 p_2 \cdots p_n} - 1$. By the fundamental theorem of arithmetic, $N$ can be written as a product of primes. Let $q$ be one such prime factor of $N$. Then $2^{p_1 p_2 \cdots p_n} \equiv 1 \pmod{q}$. Let $k$ be the order of 2 modulo $q$. This means that $k$ is the smallest positive integer such that $2^k \equiv 1 \pmod{q}$. Since $2^{p_1 p_2 \cdots p_n} \equiv 1 \pmod{q}$, we know that $k$ must divide $p_1 p_2 \cdots p_n$.  Also, by Fermat's Little Theorem, $2^{q-1} \equiv 1 \pmod{q}$, so $k$ must divide $q-1$. Therefore, $k$ must have a prime factor in common with $p_1 p_2 \cdots p_n$, say $p_i$. Because $k$ divides $q-1$, $p_i$ must also divide $q-1$. Thus, $q-1 = p_i m$ for some integer $m$. This implies $q > p_i$. Since $q$ is arbitrary, and $q$ is not equal to any of the $p_i$, there are infinitely many primes.[0m
[35mCoherence Score: 70[0m
[34mEmbedding Dissimilarity Score: 0.21[0m


