[36mQuestion: Give me a proof to Euclid's theorem of the infinitude of primes.[0m
[32mAnswer #2: Suppose for contradiction that there are only finitely many prime numbers, say $p_1, p_2, \dots, p_n$.  Consider the number $N = p_1 p_2 \cdots p_n + 1$.  Since $N$ is greater than 1, it must have a prime divisor $p$. Since $p_1, \dots, p_n$ are assumed to be all the prime numbers, $p$ must be one of them, so $p$ divides $p_1 p_2 \cdots p_n$.  But $p$ also divides $N$, so $p$ must divide the difference $N - p_1 p_2 \cdots p_n = 1$, which is impossible. Therefore, there must be infinitely many primes.[0m
