[36mQuestion: Provide a proof for the Pythagorean theorem.[0m
[32mAnswer #1: Consider a right-angled triangle with legs of length a and b, and hypotenuse of length c.  Construct four copies of this triangle and arrange them to form a large square with side length a+b.  This large square has area (a+b)².  Inside this square, there is a smaller square with side length c, and area c².  The area of the large square can also be expressed as the sum of the areas of the four triangles (4*(1/2)ab) and the smaller square (c²).  Therefore, (a+b)² = 4*(1/2)ab + c².  Expanding and simplifying, we get a² + 2ab + b² = 2ab + c², which simplifies to a² + b² = c².[0m
[35mCoherence Score: 100[0m
