[36mQuestion: Give me a proof to Euclid's theorem of the infinitude of primes.[0m
[32mAnswer #1: Assume, for the sake of contradiction, that there are only finitely many prime numbers.  Let's list them as p<sub>1</sub>, p<sub>2</sub>, ..., p<sub>n</sub>.  Consider the number N = (p<sub>1</sub> * p<sub>2</sub> * ... * p<sub>n</sub>) + 1.  N is either prime or composite. If N is prime, then we have found a prime number not in our list, contradicting our assumption. If N is composite, it must be divisible by some prime number. However, none of the primes p<sub>1</sub>, p<sub>2</sub>, ..., p<sub>n</sub> divide N (because they all leave a remainder of 1 when dividing N). Therefore, N must be divisible by a prime number not in our list, again contradicting our assumption.  Thus, there must be infinitely many prime numbers.[0m
