[32mAnswer #1: Let u and v be vectors in an inner product space.  Consider the vector w = au + bv for scalars a and b. Then ⟨w, w⟩ ≥ 0.  Let a = ⟨v,v⟩ and b = -⟨u,v⟩.  Substituting into the inequality and expanding gives ⟨v,v⟩²⟨u,u⟩ - 2⟨u,v⟩²⟨v,v⟩ + ⟨u,v⟩²⟨v,v⟩ ≥ 0.  If ⟨v,v⟩ ≠ 0, we can divide by ⟨v,v⟩ to get ⟨v,v⟩⟨u,u⟩ ≥ ⟨u,v⟩².  This is the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality. If ⟨v,v⟩ = 0, then v = 0, and the inequality holds trivially.[0m
