[36mQuestion: Give me a proof to Euclid's theorem of the infinitude of primes.[0m
[32mAnswer #1: Assume that there are only finitely many primes, p₁, p₂, …, pₙ. Consider the number Q = p₁·p₂·…·pₙ + 1. Clearly, Q is greater than 1, so it must have a prime divisor. However, none of the primes p₁, p₂, …, pₙ can divide Q because any such division would leave a remainder of 1. This contradiction shows that our original assumption is false, and therefore there must be infinitely many primes.[0m
