[36mQuestion: Give me a proof to Euclid's theorem of the infinitude of primes.[0m
[32mAnswer #3: We can prove the infinitude of primes by using a result from analytic number theory. Consider the Riemann zeta function ζ(s) defined for s > 1 as the infinite series ζ(s) = ∑ₙ₌₁∞ 1/nˢ. Euler showed that this function can also be written as an infinite product over all primes p: ζ(s) = ∏ₚ (1 – p^(–s))⁻¹. Now, take s = 1. The harmonic series ∑ₙ₌₁∞ 1/n diverges, and so the product ∏ₚ (1 – 1/p)⁻¹ must also diverge. If there were only finitely many primes, the product would be finite because it would have finitely many terms. This contradiction implies that there must be infinitely many primes.[0m
