[Model Mapping] openai/o3 -> openai/o3-mini:high
Using openai/o3 with temperature 0.7
[36mQuestion: Give me a proof to Euclid's theorem of the infinitude of primes.[0m
[32mAnswer #4: Define a topology on the set of integers where the basic open sets are all arithmetic progressions (i.e. sets of the form {a + nd : n ∈ ℤ} for fixed integers a and nonzero d). In this topology every such progression is both open and closed. For each prime p, consider the set Aₚ = {…, –2p, –p, 0, p, 2p, …}, which is closed. Notice that every integer besides 1 and –1 is divisible by at least one prime, so the set ℤ ∖ {–1, 1} equals ⋃ₚ Aₚ. If there were only finitely many primes, then ℤ ∖ {–1, 1} would be a finite union of closed sets and hence closed. This would make its complement {–1, 1} open, meaning that 1 and –1 are isolated points. However, in the arithmetic progression topology no point is isolated. This contradiction forces us to conclude that there must be infinitely many primes.[0m
